speechtext:
I assume the hon. member refers to the qualifications for training as a pilot. These are the qualifications:
To qualify for ab initio flying training in the Royal Canadian Air Force applicants must be-
(a)	Of pure European descent, sons of parents both of whom are or if deceased were at the time of their death British subjects, or naturalized British subjects;
(b)	unmarried;
(c)	medically fit for full flying duties;
(d)	between the ages of 18 and 25 on the 1st of June in the year in which he is applying for entry;
(e)	a graduate of the Royal Military College or a graduate of a four year course in the faculty of applied science, arts, arts and science, law or forestry of a recognized Canadian university;
(f)	otherwise suitable including appearance, character and personality;

The location of the twelve, which is the total number contemplated at the present time, is as follows:
No. 110, army cooperation squadron, Toronto;
No. Ill, coast artillery cooperation, Vancouver;
No. 112, army cooperation squadron, Winnipeg;
No. 113, fighter squadron, Calgary;
No. 114, bomber squadron, London, Ontario;
No. 115, fighter squadron, Montreal;
No. 116, coast artillery cooperation squadron, Halifax;
No. 117, fighter squadron, Saint John, New Brunswick;
No. 118, bomber squadron, Montreal;
No. 119, bomber squadron, Hamilton, Ontario;
No. 120, bomber squadron, Regina;
No. 121, fighter squadron, Quebec.
The three to be organized are Nos. 114 116 and 117.
